WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.380
Remember the salted cucumber?

2
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.780
Drops of water bead up on top, and nobody squeezed it.

3
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.700
So where did that water come from, and what made it leave?

4
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.920
The first film showed random hops making a one-way drift, from crowded to less

5
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.520
crowded. This time, the crowded particle is salt, and the membrane holds it back.

6
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.570
Here is the same tank and membrane.

7
00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.650
Blue water dots fill both sides.

8
00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.010
Grey salt squares sit only on the right.

9
00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.150
In this model, the membrane holds back all the salt.

10
00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.870
Real ones let a little through, much more slowly.

11
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.060
Now take a spoonful from each side.

12
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.340
On the left, every space holds water.

13
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.160
On the right, some spaces hold salt instead.

14
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:56.060
So the right side has less water.

15
00:00:57.133 --> 00:00:58.813
This grid is only a cartoon.

16
00:01:00.213 --> 00:01:03.293
We drew far more salt than is real, so you can see it.

17
00:01:04.493 --> 00:01:06.913
Real salt water is still almost all water.

18
00:01:08.093 --> 00:01:10.073
The difference is small, but it is there.

19
00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:13.577
Water dots hop at random, both ways.

20
00:01:14.997 --> 00:01:16.817
So which way will more water dots cross?

21
00:01:18.337 --> 00:01:21.177
Toward the side with more water, or toward the side with less?

22
00:01:22.537 --> 00:01:23.537
Pause and decide.

23
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:34.270
On average, more water crosses from the left, where there is more water.

24
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:40.210
Nothing pulls it. There is simply more water on the left to make the hop.

25
00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:46.683
This drift of water across a selectively permeable membrane is called osmosis.

26
00:01:48.183 --> 00:01:50.443
Water moves from more water to less water.

27
00:01:51.603 --> 00:01:54.183
That is the side with more dissolved particles.

28
00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:56.713
Back to the cucumber.

29
00:01:58.133 --> 00:01:59.133
Zoom into one cell.

30
00:02:00.573 --> 00:02:03.673
Salt sits outside, and the membrane holds most of it back.

31
00:02:04.913 --> 00:02:08.593
The salty liquid has more dissolved particles than the cell does.

32
00:02:09.353 --> 00:02:11.273
So there is less water outside.

33
00:02:12.383 --> 00:02:15.463
Water drifts out of the cell, toward the salty side.

34
00:02:16.643 --> 00:02:19.463
The vacuole shrinks, and water beads up on the slice.

35
00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:23.197
A limp lettuce leaf sits in plain water.

36
00:02:24.517 --> 00:02:29.037
Its cells hold more dissolved particles than the water around them, so there is less

37
00:02:29.037 --> 00:02:30.037
water inside.

38
00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:32.567
So water drifts in.

39
00:02:33.647 --> 00:02:37.307
The vacuole fills, and the cell presses out against its stiff wall.

40
00:02:38.507 --> 00:02:39.747
The wall presses back.

41
00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:43.307
The cells firm up, and the leaf turns crisp.

42
00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:48.137
In both cases, the cell spent no energy moving the water.

43
00:02:49.577 --> 00:02:51.297
Osmosis is passive transport.

44
00:02:52.617 --> 00:02:55.097
It is the same random drift from the first film.

45
00:02:56.417 --> 00:02:58.337
In our model, only water crosses.

46
00:02:59.567 --> 00:03:00.867
So follow the water.

47
00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:06.667
When the membrane holds the salt back, water drifts toward more dissolved particles.

48
00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:11.407
A push back, like a stiff wall, can slow or stop that drift.
